A||||7||EdsB52||Electric driven A/C???||||1||
Z||000000||EdsB52||07-27-2000||01:41 AM||||This is going to sound silly, and everyone I've talked to about this contends it's impossible, but here goes.<P>I know it is practiced to move the battery to the front compartment for many engine conversions. Another constraint is the A/C compressor being in the way. So I've been drawing up a plan to move it to the front as well. The advantages are:<P>1) more room in the engine compartment<BR>2) no need to run freon lines to and from the compressor<BR>3) Weight distribution closer to 50/50 with loss of weight in the rear, gain in the front<BR>4) less drag on the engine<BR>5) don't need to drive the idler when not using A/C<BR>6) don't have wasted freon in lines running to and from compressor<BR>7) get to run compressor at constant rpm<BR>8) don't have engine heat affecting compressor life or performance<P>I'm sure no one will dispute the numerous advantages, maybe even add a few, but the workability or feasability may be in question.<P>OK, the required parts will be an electric motor, bracket, pully system, modified A/C lines, battery cable modification, and power inverter. I wrestled with the idea of using a 12v dc motor, but found it cost prohibitive. So I ended up with the idea of a 110v ac motor and power inverter. The questions now are; how many horsepower does the A/C compressor require to be driven, and what is the optimum rpm for best performance? I could really use some help with these questions from someone who has first hand or scientific data to support the, as I don't want to spend hundreds in r & d. I know it sounds crazy, but just humor me! Thanks, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||olympic||07-27-2000||03:44 AM||||  Thats a cool idea but it ain't going to work ED.  First of all, the A/c compressor requires several hp(3-5) to turn it.  An electric motor that big would be huge.  Maybe you could mount a 5 hp Briggs and Stratton up front with it...j/k   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Also, even if you could find an a/c motor big enough you still need electricity to turn it.  Heres some math.<P>1 hp = 746 watts and the electrical efficiency of an a/c motor is maybe 65%.  So an electric motor that puts out 5 hp will draw 746*5/.65=5738 watts.  So you would need an dc/ac inverter that could put out at least that amount.  Then theres the  efficiency of the converter, say 80%.  5738/.8=7173 watts.  If your alternator puts out 14 volts it will need to produce 7173/14=512 amps just for the inverter.  So you could get rid of the a/c compressor from the rear but you would need three 200 amp alternators to replace it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Your best...???  Losers whine about doing their best.   Winners go home and f*** the prom queen! - Sean Connery in "The Rock"||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-27-2000||10:21 AM||||Conventional wisdom says it's a dumb idea.  And then, after the dumb idea is successful, conventional wisdom changes to accommodate.<P>I personally <B>like</B> the idea of a long-lasting, electrically-driven A/C compressor in automobiles.  I envision a sealed unit driven by an internal motor, like the compressors used in refrigerators.<BR>Other than being ugly and out-of-style, my 1961 model fridge works great, and it looks real swell out there on my front porch (just kidding)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !<BR> <BR>You might do a little research, and see what GM did for A/C in their electric car, the Impact.<P>When you get it all worked out, let me know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !!      ||||reg||6
Z||000003||Formula4ever||07-27-2000||11:02 AM||||Stu, your a genius! Use a compressor from a fridge pluged into an inverter. I wonder how many watts one of those compressors uses.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||07-27-2000||12:02 PM||||Yeah, I am.  And so modest, too. I say let's definitely go for the ice & water in the door thing, while we're at it.  Wait-!  Does the driver or passenger have to give up their map pocket   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ?<BR> <BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-27-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000005||Phoenix||07-27-2000||12:12 PM||||When I bought my 1970 911 (20 years ago) it had an aftermarket electrically driven air conditioner installed. It didn't work any longer and I took it out, I'll see if I can dig up any info on it as it was a dealer installed item.||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||07-27-2000||07:02 PM||||Thanks for all your input guys. I agree with Olympic that it would be electricly/mathimatically impossible, other than mounting a Briggs 5hp gas motor and making a hybrid vehicle. Stugood's idea of a refrigerator A/C modular unit sounds good and might work considering the small loop that would be required for the system. I think the pressure and efficiency might be in question though. The required amperage and wattage wouldn't be infeasable for a refrigerator system in my opinion. It is definately worth looking into though. And yes, Phoenix, if you have any info about a produced unit that we could copy, that would be great. Thanks again and let's keep brainstorming.<P>P.S. Does it really sound that ridiculous to mount a 5hp Briggs motor run by propane in the front compartment? If the motor had electric start and a tank large enough to last for several days, would it be so awefull? If the gas motor was easily removable, for winter non-operation, would it be so ackward? Tell me what you think.||||reg||3
Z||000007||jlruiz67||07-27-2000||08:41 PM||||How about a heat pump? I was also thinking about the GM electric car that has A/C. I really dig the idea of having a complete A/C unit in front and have more space in the trunk plus less moving parts, hoses and freon. But I like to explore more of the heat pump consept and see where it goes. ||||reg||1
